        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "8cbc5021-a1f1-3a65-baa5-c4f5d38157d7",
                "word": "foncteur",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "ddfb964d-762f-4c61-a7d1-ffdef003f81e",
                        "entry_id": "8cbc5021-a1f1-3a65-baa5-c4f5d38157d7",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "foncteurs"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "171ec070-ef14-3fa0-97ad-3e1de9fe533a",
                        "entry_id": "8cbc5021-a1f1-3a65-baa5-c4f5d38157d7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Mot exprimant la relation grammaticale entre les autres mots d’une phrase, comme par exemple les conjonctions, les prépositions, etc."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c6142b0c-ecd6-4e96-982d-09aa092667cd",
                                "sense_id": "171ec070-ef14-3fa0-97ad-3e1de9fe533a",
                                "text": "Il me semble que nous trouvons là la raison pour laquelle les foncteurs logiques « tous » et « quelques » sont des bloqueurs forts, ce qui a, nous l’avons vu, des conséquences forts intéressantes pour leurs relations de portée par rapport à la narration."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "97fce46b-0871-3ca9-a300-cbde0ac50530",
                        "entry_id": "8cbc5021-a1f1-3a65-baa5-c4f5d38157d7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Application qui permet de définir une relation complexe entre les morphismes des catégories mathématiques tout en préservant la structure interne des catégories prises séparément."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b9d1258-afc9-43a5-99ec-48334ebef08d",
                                "sense_id": "97fce46b-0871-3ca9-a300-cbde0ac50530",
                                "text": "Un foncteur F:𝒞→𝒟 associe à tout objet et à tout morphisme de la catégorie 𝒞, un unique objet et un unique morphisme de la catégorie 𝒟."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "75eebeb1-b138-36ff-9e26-1e741b148c94",
                        "entry_id": "8cbc5021-a1f1-3a65-baa5-c4f5d38157d7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Entité qui réunit les qualités d’objet et de fonction dans le cadre d’une programmation par objets."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Programmation orientée objet"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "34c64b6b-9265-4217-aa0f-fc2232152b4c",
                                "sense_id": "75eebeb1-b138-36ff-9e26-1e741b148c94",
                                "text": "Pour cela nous avons besoin d’un objet fonction (on dit parfois fonction objet ou foncteur), c’est-à-dire d’un objet qui se comporte comme une fonction."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "bba49c79-b571-4269-a45c-b71f4c27eba2",
                        "source_entry_id": "8cbc5021-a1f1-3a65-baa5-c4f5d38157d7",
                        "source_sense_id": "171ec070-ef14-3fa0-97ad-3e1de9fe533a",
                        "source_sense_description": "Sens linguistique",
                        "target_lang_word": "functor",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "functor",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "012863eb-1be6-3634-8f2b-e525da1d3281",
                                        "entry_id": "eccc8de4-9d98-3a49-8d78-5f7252f9da92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A function word."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9166bd28-fdbd-37ac-b344-a128dd1dc316",
                                        "entry_id": "eccc8de4-9d98-3a49-8d78-5f7252f9da92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A function object."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c42d4ee7-9dff-3ff3-ac02-9fc1df1fcaee",
                                        "entry_id": "eccc8de4-9d98-3a49-8d78-5f7252f9da92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A category homomorphism; a morphism from a source category to a target category which maps objects to objects and arrows to arrows (either covariantly or contravariantly), in such a way as to preserve morphism composition and identities."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fbe01d0f-1ca5-4c36-9850-f10269c9dc5f",
                                                "sense_id": "c42d4ee7-9dff-3ff3-ac02-9fc1df1fcaee",
                                                "text": "In the category of categories, #92;mathbb#123;CAT#125;, the objects are categories and the morphisms are functors."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cf740a16-c389-499d-879f-c2fd87643571",
                                                "sense_id": "c42d4ee7-9dff-3ff3-ac02-9fc1df1fcaee",
                                                "text": "1991, Natalie Wadhwa (translator), Yu. A. Brudnyǐ, N. Ya. Krugljak, Interpolation Functors and Interpolation Spaces, Volume I, Elsevier (North-Holland), page 143,\nChoosing for U the operation of closure, regularization or relative completion, we obtain from a given functor ℱ∈𝒥ℱ the functors\n◌̅F: overrightarrow X→◌̅F( overrightarrow X),F⁰: overrightarrow X→F( overrightarrow X)⁰,Fᶜ: overrightarrow X→F( overrightarrow X)ᶜ."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d4589548-0f29-4ef6-83b6-1da710d0d620",
                                                "sense_id": "c42d4ee7-9dff-3ff3-ac02-9fc1df1fcaee",
                                                "text": "Given a homotopical category X and a functor u#58;A#92;rightarrowB, a homotopical u-colimit (resp. u-limit) functor on X will be a homotopically terminal (resp. initial) Kan extension of the identity (50.2) along the induced diagram functor Xᵘ#58;Xᴮ#92;rightarrowXᴬ (47.1)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b50fd3d5-6b13-4e02-a161-5bd77a035055",
                                                "sense_id": "c42d4ee7-9dff-3ff3-ac02-9fc1df1fcaee",
                                                "text": "2009, Benoit Fresse, Modules Over Operads and Functors, Springer, Lecture Notes in Mathematics: 1967, page 35,\nIn this chapter, we recall the definition of the category of Σ_*-objects and we review the relationship between Σ_*-objects and functors. In short, a Σ_*-object (in English words, a symmetric sequence of objects, or simply a symmetric object) is the coefficient sequence of a generalized symmetric functor S(M):X→S(M,X), defined by a formula of the form\nS(M,X)=⨁ ᪲ᵣ₌₀(M(r)⊗X)_(Σᵣ)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5e6d474d-a46f-31c9-b3f2-fbe56ad85a5e",
                                        "entry_id": "eccc8de4-9d98-3a49-8d78-5f7252f9da92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A structure allowing a function to apply within a generic type, in a way that is conceptually similar to a functor in category theory."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6055dcec-4a5a-40fb-85d3-a2cfc1f37759",
                        "source_entry_id": "8cbc5021-a1f1-3a65-baa5-c4f5d38157d7",
